644:   /auth/login:
645:     post:
646:       tags: [auth]
647:       summary: Log in with username and password
648:       description: |-
649:         On success, the servlet session id is rotated, a fresh CSRF token
650:         is issued, and the rate-limit buckets for the caller IP and the
651:         target user are cleared.
652: 
653:         Rate limiting has two scopes. The per-IP gate returns `429
654:         rate_limited` with a `Retry-After` header. The per-user gate is
655:         scoped to `(client IP, username)`; when it trips, the response is a
656:         generic `401 auth_required` with no `Retry-After` — identical to a
657:         credential rejection — so it does not disclose per-user rate-limit
658:         state.
659: 
660:         An already-authenticated session does not short-circuit; the
661:         supplied credentials are always verified.
662: 
663:         `return_to` must be a relative path matching
664:         `^/[A-Za-z0-9_\-/.?&=%:@+~#*!,;]*$`, and additionally must not be
665:         protocol-relative (no leading `//`) or contain ASCII control
666:         characters. Values that do not pass the allowlist are silently
667:         dropped from the echoed response.
668: 
669:         Unlike the other state-changing endpoints, this endpoint folds
670:         oversized request bodies and unsupported `Content-Type` headers
671:         into `400 invalid_request` (a deliberate simplification — clients
672:         should treat any 4xx body as a client error and inspect
673:         `error.code`). The other endpoints emit distinct `413` and `415`
674:         statuses.
